# Privy's packaging is broken, and this is the one-line consequence.
#
# @privy-io/react-auth imports @stripe/crypto in its FiatOnrampScreen without
# declaring it as a dependency, a peer, or an optional. @stripe/crypto in turn
# declares a peer of @stripe/stripe-js@^1.46, and the current stripe-js is 9.x.
# So npm's strict peer resolution refuses the tree outright:
#
#   Could not resolve dependency:
#   peer @stripe/stripe-js@"^1.46.0" from @stripe/crypto@1.1.3
#
# Nothing in cipher uses Stripe. Both packages exist only so Privy's own import
# resolves — see the trap already written down in CLAUDE.md. The conflict is
# therefore between two packages we never call, about a screen we never show.
#
# This file rather than an install flag on the host: a flag set in Vercel's
# dashboard is invisible from the repo, and the next person to clone this and
# run `npm install` on a clean machine would hit the identical error with
# nothing to explain it. Committed, it fixes the build everywhere at once and
# says why.
#
# Delete it the day Privy declares its dependencies properly.
legacy-peer-deps=true
